import equational_theories.Definability.LinearTargets

/-!
# The remaining targets, refuted over an arbitrary domain

`Definability/LinearTargets.lean` did this for the seventeen "squaring" targets, whose eliminant is
`s * (s - 1)` times one of three fixed cofactors. That list was chosen by hand; the rest of the
board turns out to have the same shape, with other cofactors, so this file drops the restriction
and generates a lemma for every open target whose coefficient system eliminates.

For a target the coefficient equations eliminate to one polynomial `F` in the coefficient sum
`s = α + β`. Factor it over `ℤ`. `Magma.LinShift c` writes `s = 1 + c * γ`, so for each factor `f`
other than `s - 1`,

  `f (1 + c * γ) = f 1 + c * (an explicit polynomial in γ)`,

and the factor is impossible as soon as `f 1` is not in the ideal `(c)`. Every factor therefore
contributes a hypothesis of the same shape, `∀ x, n + c * x ≠ 0` with `n = f 1`: `n = 1` says `c` is
not a unit, `n = 3` is the `h₃` of the earlier file, and the rest ask the same of `5`, `7` or `11`.
One residue map `ℤ[θ] → 𝔽ₚ` killing `c` decides all of them at once, since it sends `f (1 + c * γ)`
to `f 1`.

The factor `s - 1` is the exception: there the coefficient sum carries no information and the
system collapses to a univariate `P α`, so it needs its own hypothesis, that `P` has no root.

Being clone arguments over infinite models, the certificates built from these refute
`TermDefinableFrom`, and only over all magmas.

This file is generated by `genlintgt2.py`.
-/

open Law Law.MagmaLaw

namespace Magma

